  "subject": "An account executive role in Bengaluru, and why I thought of you",
  "text": "Hi Arjun.

You told me I could send you roles beyond the one you came to me for. This is one of those.

A seed-stage software company in Bengaluru is hiring an account executive. The job is the full sales cycle: building outbound pipeline from scratch, running demos, and closing.

I thought of you because you were the first sales hire at Northwind and ran the full sales cycle at Lakeside before that. That is from your own CV, not a guess of mine.

The facts that usually decide it:

- On site, five days a week.
- They are asking for three to five years of selling B2B software.

Three questions, and short answers are fine:

1. Are you looking at the moment?
2. Does this role interest you?
3. If yes to both, may I take the next step with you?

The next step is not your name going to them. They have set a high bar on deal size and targets, so I would come back with four specific questions first. I only put someone forward when I can back them with their own numbers, and I will tell you straight if I cannot.

I am not naming the company in this email. You will know exactly who it is before your name goes anywhere near them, and you can stop at that point.

If it is a no, say so and that is the end of it. If I do not hear back, nothing happens and nothing is held against you.

Charlie",
}
`;

